  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Strongest reducing agent has the highest tendency to get oxidised. Higher the oxidation potential, higher is the tendency to get oxidised. Reduction potential of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;is least (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Cr</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.74</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>) which indicates that oxidation potential of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math>&nbsp;is highest (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><mi>Cr</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>.74</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>). Thus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math>&nbsp;is strongest reducing agent.</p></blockquote>",
